sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch09-11> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The apparently sudden appearance of whole groups of allied species in a formation is generally an artefact: such groups existed and multiplied earlier, elsewhere or during unrecorded intervals, so older fossils of them should continue to be found.";
    dc:type "prediction";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Apparent sudden origin of groups reflects missing earlier records";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter IX, “On the Imperfection of the Geological Record”; section “On the sudden appearance of groups of species”. Scope: first appearances of genera, families and higher groups in the fossil record"
}
